comments.body:
Not totally late to the party @mgargiullo, well I think you have a good point and it could be acceptable having the wildcard TLS cert in dev, test and staging, but never have the same thing in production. I do understand that some organizations prefer it this way for easy management of certificates. 

While you are correct, using wildcard TLS certificates are not inherently insecure, but the management of these certificates are mostly poor in non-mature organizations. They seldom rotate or revoke those and every deployment has the same certificate which is only a problem in the event of a compromised certificate (attack vectors here would either if someone who has former access to these certificates left the company and has a malicious intent or a server breach). Yes, the article sounds like it is pushing for Let's Encrypt (please ignore this ad part as I cannot find a good reference for this issue). 

@jmanico, @elarlang, I am no expert in TLS certificates either, but I think this is a good addition, so I will be drafting something for the PR. Let me think deeper if I miss something to consider all cases. I will be having the PR soon.
